  <language>en_US</language>
  <keyword>nanmin</keyword>
  <short_description>Minimum, ignoring NaN values.</short_description>
  <syntax>
    <syntax_item>y = nanmin(X)</syntax_item>
    <syntax_item>[y, idx] = nanmin(X)</syntax_item>
    <syntax_item>y = nanmin(X, Y)</syntax_item>
    <syntax_item>[y, idx] = nanmin(X, [], dim)</syntax_item>
  </syntax>
  <description>
    <p><b>nanmin</b> returns the minimum after removing <b>NaN</b> values; a slice made only of <b>NaN</b> yields <b>NaN</b>.</p>
    <p><b>nanmin(X, Y)</b> returns the element-wise minimum of <b>X</b> and <b>Y</b>, ignoring <b>NaN</b>.</p>
    <p>The optional second output <b>idx</b> holds the indices of the minima. It is equivalent to <b>min(X, ..., 'omitnan')</b>.</p>
  </description>
  <examples>
    <example_item>
      <example_item_type>nelson</example_item_type>
      <example_item_description />
      <example_item_data runnable="cli"><![CDATA[[y, idx] = nanmin([4 NaN 1 NaN 3])]]></example_item_data>
    </example_item>
  </examples>
